          </div>

          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, tub size matters enormously on the flagship: the XL tub of Protein + Recovery costs about 40% less per gram of protein than the standard tub. If you have tried it and know you like it, buying small is just paying extra. Second, Essential Protein is roughly half the cost per gram of the flagship's standard tub, because you are not paying for the certification, the probiotic or the tart cherry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Single-ingredient pea isolates are in a different price bracket altogether. Naked Pea in its largest bag works out to well under a dollar per 25 g at list price, a third of what you pay for Vega's flagship in a standard tub. You give up flavor, the seed protein blend and NSF Certified for Sport, and in return you save a lot of money over a year of daily shakes. Our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein guide</a> covers that trade-off in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            A daily shake makes the gap concrete. At one serving a day, the standard Protein + Recovery tub costs about $100 a month at list price. The XL tub costs about $60, Essential about $40, and an unflavored pea isolate about $25. None of those numbers is outrageous for a protein source, but they are different enough that you should know which column you are buying from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Certifications and Testing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Certification is the clearest reason to pay Vega prices. Protein + Recovery carries <strong>NSF Certified for Sport</strong>. According to Vega's product page, that program screens for more than 270 substances banned in sport, checks that the label matches the contents, and audits the manufacturing facility twice a year. If you compete in a tested sport, a certified product is the only sensible choice, and few plant proteins this widely available carry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein + Creatine is certified under <strong>Informed Choice</strong>, a different but also credible program run by LGC that tests for substances on the World Anti-Doping Agency list. Essential Protein and Protein + Supergreens show no sport certification on Vega's site. They are labelled vegan, gluten-free and non-GMO, which tells you about sourcing, not about contaminant or banned-substance testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are the question plant protein buyers ask most, and it is a fair one. Plants take up lead, cadmium and arsenic from soil, so pea, rice and seed proteins tend to test higher than whey. NSF's program includes contaminant screening, which is part of why the certified flagship is worth more than the uncertified tubs. For the wider picture, our guide on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains what the testing does and does not cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical rule: certification is granted per product and sometimes per flavor, not per brand. Before you rely on it, search the product and flavor on the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice database. That takes a minute, and it is the only way to be sure the tub in your cart is the version that was tested. The same rule applies to every brand on this site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Taste, Texture and Mixing: What Buyers Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted these products, so this section summarizes what buyers and retailer reviews consistently say. The common theme is that Vega mixes thinner and less gritty than many plant proteins, particularly in a shaker with cold water. That is the main reason people pay more for it than for an unflavored pea isolate, which most buyers describe as thick and earthy unless it goes into a blender.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common complaint is stevia. A share of reviewers find the aftertaste strong in water, especially in vanilla, and say it improves a lot in almond or oat milk, or blended with fruit. Some long-time Sport Premium buyers also say the flavor changed at some point. Vega says the renamed Protein + Recovery is the same product, so treat those reports as anecdotal, but they are worth knowing if you are rebuying from memory.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate and mocha are generally the safest flavors in buyer feedback, because cocoa masks the earthy edge plant protein can have. Berry splits opinion more. Essential Protein is usually described as creamier than the Sport line, which fits its ingredient list: coconut oil powder and acacia gum are there for mouthfeel. Supergreens draws mixed reviews. Some buyers like it in smoothies, while others find the greens noticeable in water.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a better shake from any of them, use 12 oz of very cold liquid, shake longer than you would with whey, and let it sit for a minute so the gums can hydrate. If you plan to blend with frozen fruit, the cheaper Essential tub tends to make more sense, because fruit covers most flavor differences. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">smoothie protein guide</a> has more on what blends well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Vega vs Other Plant Protein Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Garden of Life Sport</strong>, Vega is closer than most comparisons. Both put about 30 g of protein in a certified sport product. Garden of Life adds organic certification, while Vega tends to win on availability and buyer-reported drinkability. Our <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life protein review</a> goes through that line in detail. If USDA Organic matters to you, Garden of Life gets the nod. If you want the easiest shake to drink in water, buyer feedback tends to favor Vega.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Orgain</strong>, Vega is the more serious training product. Orgain's plant powder is cheaper per tub, but its standard serving has about 21 g of protein, so the real cost per 25 g is closer than it looks. Orgain also sweetens with erythritol and stevia, and erythritol bothers some stomachs. Our <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a> covers it. Vega's flagship wins on protein per scoop and certification. Orgain wins on grocery-store price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <strong>OWYN</strong> and <strong>Sunwarrior</strong>, the deciding factor is usually allergens and diet. OWYN is built around being free of the top allergens, and Sunwarrior leans on fermented and raw-food positioning. See our <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">OWYN review</a> and <a href="/sunwarrior-protein-powder-review/">Sunwarrior review</a>. Vega's shared-facility statement means OWYN is the safer pick for someone with serious food allergies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against plain pea isolate, the trade is flavor and certification against cost. If you blend every shake with fruit and milk, the flavor advantage mostly disappears and an unflavored isolate saves you a lot. If you drink your protein from a shaker at the gym and need it tested, Vega's flagship is the better buy. That is the honest reason it remains one of the default recommendations in our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powders</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy Vega, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy Protein + Recovery</strong> if you are a tested athlete, a lifter who wants 30 g of plant protein in one scoop, or someone who needs a shaker-friendly vegan shake for the gym bag. Buy the XL tub once you know you like the flavor. It is the version of Vega with the clearest reason to cost more than the competition, and it is the one we would put in a cart first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy Protein + Creatine</strong> only if you already take 3-5 g of creatine a day and want to simplify. It replaces two products with one and is cheaper per gram of protein than the flagship's standard tub. Skip it if you want a second shake later in the day, because doubling the protein doubles the creatine. That is not dangerous for most healthy adults, but it is wasteful.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy Essential Protein</strong> if you want Vega's flavor in daily smoothies or oats and do not need certification. It is the best value in the range, with no added sugar and a modest ingredient list. <strong>Skip Protein + Supergreens</strong> unless you specifically like the idea. The greens are a small dose and the tubs are small, so you pay a premium for something a handful of spinach does better.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Vega entirely</strong> if you are on a tight budget and happy to blend, if you have a severe soy, peanut or tree nut allergy, or if stevia gives you a headache or an aftertaste you hate. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or take medication that affects protein or creatine use, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding any protein powder. This page is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Get the Most From a Vega Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dose by protein, not by scoop. Research on muscle protein synthesis suggests that most adults get the most out of a serving of roughly 20-40 g of protein, with plant proteins sitting toward the higher end because they have slightly less leucine than whey. That is why the 30 g Protein + Recovery scoop is well judged and why a 20 g serving of any plant powder is better paired with other protein in the same meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters less than total intake. A shake within a couple of hours of training is convenient, but your total daily protein does most of the work. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein calculator guide</a> shows how to work out a daily target, and our guide on <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers timing. For most people, one Vega shake a day closes the gap between what they eat and what they need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store it properly. Plant powders with probiotics and seed proteins keep best sealed, cool and dry. Vega says the Bacillus subtilis strain it uses is heat-stable, but that is a reason not to worry about shipping, not permission to leave the tub open next to the stove. Use the scoop that comes in the tub, as scoop sizes differ between Vega products and borrowing another brand's scoop throws your numbers off.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are switching from whey, give it a couple of weeks. Some people notice more fullness or a change in digestion when they move to pea-based protein, partly because of the fiber in blends like Essential and Supergreens. If one product does not suit you, try the flagship before you write off plant protein. Its ingredient list is the shortest in the range. For how whey and plant proteins compare overall, see our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> breakdown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the standard tub of the flagship forever</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 44-serving XL tub of Protein + Recovery costs roughly 40% less per gram of protein than the 19-serving tub at Vega's list prices. Small tubs make sense for a first try. After that, paying the small-tub premium every month adds up to a meaningful sum over a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Vega tub is NSF certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Only Protein + Recovery carries NSF Certified for Sport, and Protein + Creatine uses Informed Choice instead. Essential and Supergreens show no sport certification on Vega's site. If you are drug tested, check the exact product and flavor in the certifier's database before you buy, rather than trusting the brand name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the sugar in Protein + Creatine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The flagship has no added sugar, so it is easy to assume the creatine version matches it. It does not. Protein + Creatine lists organic cane sugar along with stevia. It is not a large amount, but anyone tracking sugar or switching for a clean label should read the panel first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Supergreens as a vegetable serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The greens in Protein + Supergreens are a small blend added to a protein shake. They do not replace eating vegetables, and you pay a noticeable premium per gram of protein for them. If you want greens, add a handful of spinach to a cheaper tub in the blender.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking it like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plant blends with xanthan or acacia gum need more liquid and more shaking than whey. Use about 12 oz of very cold liquid, shake well, and let the shake rest for a minute. Buyers who mix it in 6 oz of warm water are the ones who report a gritty, chalky drink.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Vega Sport Premium Protein discontinued?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not exactly. Vega renamed it Protein + Recovery and says the formula is unchanged: 30 g of protein from pea, pumpkin seed and sunflower seed, with tart cherry and a probiotic. Retailers sometimes still list it under the old name. The label at the time of writing still shows NSF Certified for Sport, but check the flavor in NSF's database.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Vega protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the tub. Protein + Recovery and Protein + Creatine have 30 g per scoop. Essential Protein and Protein + Supergreens have 25 g per serving, and Essential takes two scoops to get there. All-in-One Nutrition has 20 g. These figures come from the label at the time of writing and vary slightly by flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Vega use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The flagship Protein + Recovery, Essential Protein and Supergreens are sweetened with stevia leaf extract and have no added sugar, per Vega. Protein + Creatine is different: its label lists organic cane sugar as well as stevia. None of the range uses sucralose, aspartame or acesulfame potassium, according to the ingredient lists at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Vega protein good for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, if you take enough. A 30 g scoop of Protein + Recovery provides a dose that research suggests is enough to stimulate muscle protein synthesis in most adults, and the blend has a complete amino acid profile. Muscle growth still depends mostly on training and total daily protein. The powder just makes your daily target easier to hit.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Vega protein contain heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  All plant proteins contain trace heavy metals from soil, and Vega does not publish batch results on its product pages. Protein + Recovery's NSF Certified for Sport status includes contaminant screening, which is the best assurance in the range. If heavy metals are your priority, choose the certified product and check the flavor in NSF's database before you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Vega protein good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help, as any protein powder can, because protein is filling and a 170-190 calorie shake can replace a less filling snack. Essential Protein is the most sensible choice for this at about 170 calories for 25 g. No powder causes weight loss by itself. Your overall calorie intake and diet quality decide that.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who owns Vega now?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Vega was founded in Canada, bought by WhiteWave Foods in 2015, and became part of Danone when Danone acquired WhiteWave. In June 2021 Danone sold Vega to funds managed by the US private equity firm WM Partners, and terms were not disclosed. The core products continued, with some renamed in later years.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

